        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "My previous rationale for leaving out those popup (not popmenu) patches is to rely on `runtime/doc/` for  documentation on possible \"high-level\" features or behavior that Nvim may port to enhance its own implementation. It's not really for \"compatibility\"; it's like a ticket for deferred technical decision. If you say that none of them, even in spirit, are relevant, then they're N/A.\r\nSame rationale for \"high-level features\" that are not coupled with Vimscript `eval`.\r\nI'll update vim-patch.sh and `*hunk*` files to auto-N/A them. \r\n\r\nAs for popupmenu, I tried to port some a long time ago but gave up because of failing tests. At this point, I treat it like `drawscreen.c`, `highlight`, `syntax`, and similar files because it's difficult to analyze where to \"inject\" the applicable changes, if there are any. Unless there's a test or crash dump (from an immediate follow-up patch), it's simpler to treat the source code changes as N/A.\r\n\r\nAs for Windows, I thought `resolve` and `reparse` and similar patches are handling edge cases beyond the standard filepath issues that the refactor covered. If the new tests pass after the refactor, then yes the Vim implementation is trivially N/A.\r\n\r\nOS-patches at this point are grey-area to me; if you initiated marking these N/A, I won't oppose. Many changes should be N/A (ie. vtp/vcon truecolor, signals, jobs, terminal) after being owned fully by Nvim. I didn't implement the Windows screen changes (and had to revert back to 0.11.x at some point during work).",
